<spell-notebook "sticky theming">
	<header level="1">
		Sticky
		<header>Sticky content stays fixed to the viewport while its container is on screen.</>
	</>
	<sticky-tab-bar showing="theming"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#tokens">Tokens</>
			<item link="#themes">Themes</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- THEMING:  the family's tokens, live, plus how to set them and which themes touch it. -->
	<section label="Tokens">
		<markdown>
			Theme with the public `--ui-sticky-*` tokens on the sticky or any ancestor; `::part(sticky)` works too. Style the
			box through `::part(sticky)`, and a stuck one through `:state(stuck)`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```css
			ui-sticky {
			  --ui-sticky-z-index: 50;
			}
			ui-sticky:state(stuck)::part(sticky) {
			  background: var(--ui-surface);
			}
			```
		</>
		<message state="info" size="small">
			<markdown>
				The offsets come from the `offset` / `bottom-offset` attributes; `--ui-sticky-offset` /
				`--ui-sticky-bottom-offset` are for class-grammar markup only. Never set the `--_ui-sticky-*` names: the element
				writes them inline from its attributes.
			</>
		</>
		<markdown>Edit a value below to try it.</>
		<html>
			<ui-docs-tokens family="ui-sticky" playground>
				<div style="height: 14em; overflow: auto; border: 1px solid var(--ui-border-color)" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Sticky rail example">
					<div style="display: flex; gap: 1em; padding: 0 1em">
						<div style="flex: 1">
							<p>Fomantic's sticky kept content in view with scroll listeners and fixed positioning. Here the browser does it: the box is <code>position: sticky</>, and the element only reports when it is stuck.</>
							<p>Fomantic's sticky kept content in view with scroll listeners and fixed positioning. Here the browser does it: the box is <code>position: sticky</>, and the element only reports when it is stuck.</>
							<p>Fomantic's sticky kept content in view with scroll listeners and fixed positioning. Here the browser does it: the box is <code>position: sticky</>, and the element only reports when it is stuck.</>
							<p>Fomantic's sticky kept content in view with scroll listeners and fixed positioning. Here the browser does it: the box is <code>position: sticky</>, and the element only reports when it is stuck.</>
							<p>Fomantic's sticky kept content in view with scroll listeners and fixed positioning. Here the browser does it: the box is <code>position: sticky</>, and the element only reports when it is stuck.</>
						</>
						<div style="width: 8em">
							<ui-sticky offset="8">
								<p style="margin: 0; padding: 0.5em; background: var(--ui-surface-strong)">I stick 8px from the top</>
							</>
						</>
					</>
				</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Themes">
		<markdown>A sticky has no look of its own, only whatever it holds.</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
